// ============================================================================
// CONFIG-RESOLUTION · PROPERTIES / YAML  (a key's value naming a class)
//
// value-interpretation.dl built the merged key space and the placeholder chain.
// This file reads the VALUES: a config value that names a handler class is a real
// edge into code the source never mentions —
//     spring.kafka.consumer.value-deserializer=com.acme.OrderDeserializer
//     app.pipeline.handler: com.acme.EnrichStage
// The container reflects that name into an instance and calls it.
//
// SHAPE TEST, NOT A GUESS. Two independent gates must BOTH pass before a value is
// reported as a class reference:
//   (1) the KEY ends in a suffix that means "a class goes here" (cfg_handler_key_suffix)
//       OR the value resolves to an actual type in this program;
//   (2) the VALUE is FQN-shaped.
// Gate (1)'s second arm is what stops the suffix list from having to be complete:
// any value that names a type we can SEE is reported regardless of its key. Gate
// (1)'s first arm is what lets an UNRESOLVED value still be reported as an unknown
// (we know a class belonged there and we could not find it).
// ============================================================================

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. "this key expects a class"
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A key's LAST dotted segment, so the suffix knobs match `a.b.handler` on "handler".
cfg_key_dot(k, i) :- config_key_defined(k), contains(".", k),
    i = range(0, strlen(k)), substr(k, i, 1) = ".".
cfg_key_dot_last(k, m) :- cfg_key_dot(k, _), m = max i : cfg_key_dot(k, i).
cfg_key_leaf(k, s) :- cfg_key_dot_last(k, m), s = substr(k, m + 1, strlen(k) - m - 1).
cfg_key_leaf(k, k) :- config_key_defined(k), !contains(".", k).
cfg_key_expects_class(k) :- cfg_key_leaf(k, s), cfg_handler_key_suffix(s).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 2. VALUE -> TYPE
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
cfg_name_demand(v) :- config_key_value(_, _, v), cfg_fqn_shaped(v).

config_class_ref(fmt, k, v, t, prov) :- config_key_value(fmt, k, v),
    cfg_fqn_shaped(v), cfg_name_type(v, prov, t).

// DECLARED UNKNOWN — only where the KEY said a class belonged (gate 1a). A
// FQN-shaped value under an arbitrary key is far more often a package name, a
// logger category or a JNDI path than a broken class reference, and reporting
// those would bury the real ones.
config_unresolved(fmt, k, v, "unresolved_class") :- config_key_value(fmt, k, v),
    cfg_key_expects_class(k), cfg_fqn_shaped(v), !cfg_name_resolved(v).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 3. PLACEHOLDER UNKNOWNS
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A key referenced by a placeholder but defined in no config file this analysis
// saw. Legitimate at runtime (an env var, a -D flag, a config server, a profile
// file outside the scan) — which is exactly why it must be DECLARED rather than
// dropped: it is the boundary of what the config graph can see.
config_unresolved("placeholder", k, ref, "undefined_key") :-
    config_key_ref(k, ref, ""), !config_key_defined(ref).
// A placeholder WITH a default still terminates, so it is deliberately NOT reported
// above: config_key_ref already carries the default in its third column, and any
// consumer that cares can read it there. A separate relation restating that would
// have no consumer — the defect this whole phase exists to remove.
